WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.660
AN UTTER FAIL:
$7.6 billion, 7,800 jobs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:09.420
and 3 years of time. This is how much Microsoft&nbsp;
lost on their worst acquisition of all time:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:15.060
Nokia. From an outside perspective, this was&nbsp;
an obvious disaster just waiting to happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:20.340
Microsoft had taken a gamble with a legacy phone&nbsp;
maker that was struggling to keep up with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:25.320
modern world of smartphones. And unfortunately for&nbsp;
Microsoft, things didn’t play out in their favor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:31.560
But what if I told you that there was more to this&nbsp;
story than just a misguided acquisition with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:37.440
bunch of money. You see, the bleak state of Nokia&nbsp;
isn’t just obvious in hindsight. It was obvious in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:43.440
real time as well. In fact, investors were largely&nbsp;
against the acquisition. The day that the deal was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:50.160
finalized, Microsoft shares fell 6% losing the&nbsp;
company $15 billion in market value or over twice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:57.300
the price of Nokia. Why you ask? Well, the writing&nbsp;
was already on the wall for Nokia. Not only was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.940
their market share and brand presence collapsing&nbsp;
but they had lost $4 billion the previous year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:10.140
So Microsoft was having to massively subsidize&nbsp;
their operations just to own the company. Hearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.580
this, you’re probably thinking that Microsoft&nbsp;
must’ve been real desperate to get into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.680
phone market, and you’re right, they were&nbsp;
real desparate but what if I told you that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.600
there was more to this story. What if I told&nbsp;
you that Microsoft was actually blackmailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.820
into buying Nokia? Nothing straight up illegal&nbsp;
went down but Nokia very much leveraged their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:35.040
position and power over Microsoft within&nbsp;
the phone market to force a $7.2 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.540
deal down their throats. So, join me as&nbsp;
we take a look at how one of the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:44.218
companies in the world got blackmailed into&nbsp;
making the worst acquisition of their life.

00:01:44.218 --> 00:01:49.560
A DESPERATE PLUNGE:&nbsp;

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:58.320
Taking a look back, Microsoft’s foray into the&nbsp;
smartphone industry can actually be traced all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.720
the way back to early 2004 to a project&nbsp;
name Photon. The goal of Photon was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:09.360
create a mobile version of Windows that would&nbsp;
be launched by the end of 2007. To be honest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:15.240
Photon didn’t look all that great but contrary to&nbsp;
popular belief Microsoft was very much ahead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:21.240
the curve with smartphones. The problem though was&nbsp;
that they didn’t stay ahead of the curve. Photon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:26.580
ended up being one of those projects that kept&nbsp;
being delayed time after time because frankly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.380
Photon was just a side project for Microsoft&nbsp;
that they didn’t really care about. That was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:37.200
until 2007 when Apple would reveal the iPhone. On&nbsp;
the outside, Microsoft would put up a brave front&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.200
and act like they didn’t care about the iPhone. In&nbsp;
fact, Steve Ballmer, the CEO of Microsoft at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.120
time would actually laugh the iPhone and claim&nbsp;
that it didn’t appeal to business customers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:07.440
But while Microsoft acted like they didn’t care&nbsp;
for PR purposes, internally, they were scrambling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:13.440
They would reorganize their entire mobile team&nbsp;
and start working on a “new operating system”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.020
This was basically code for putting lipstick&nbsp;
on Photon and shipping it as the next big thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:25.380
but this itself took way longer than expected. In&nbsp;
fact, they would end up rushing a beta version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:32.280
this in 2009 dubbed Windows Mobile 6.5. Microsoft&nbsp;
was very much hoping that the Windows name would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:38.700
carry this product but that simply wasn’t enough.&nbsp;
Users would see right through this crappy software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:44.280
and even Steven Ballmer would end up admitting&nbsp;
that they screwed up. You would think that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.060
failure would lead to Microsoft reevaluating&nbsp;
their position and approaching the smartphone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:54.540
market from a different approach but Microsoft&nbsp;
simply doubled down. You see, Microsoft very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.400
had tunnel vision. All they could see was that&nbsp;
Apple and Google already had solid products on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:05.160
the market while they didn’t. So, their number one&nbsp;
goal was to just to get onto the market as soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:11.340
as possible and unfortunately, this led to yet&nbsp;
another crappy launch with Windows Phone 7. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:15.900
the announcement, Microsoft would proudly tell the&nbsp;
world that they had struck deals with many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:23.640
world’s largest manufacturers including Dell,&nbsp;
Garmin, Asus, HTC, HP, LG, Samsung, Sony, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:29.880
Toshiba. Microsoft was so confident in the Windows&nbsp;
Phone 7 that they would actually stage a funeral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:35.400
for the iPhone. But when the Windows Phone 7&nbsp;
actually made it to the market, it was clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:41.820
Microsoft had cut a lot corners. The phone didn’t&nbsp;
support copy pasting, custom ringtones, tethering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.680
multi tasking or even traditional notifications.&nbsp;
Microsoft didn’t think they needed notifications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:52.320
because of their cool little windows tiles but&nbsp;
the public didn’t agree and they weren’t the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:58.380
only ones. After seeing how Windows Phone 7 turned&nbsp;
out, half of Microsoft’s hardware partners would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:03.660
back out of their deal. And honestly, I think only&nbsp;
reason they other half moved forward was to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:09.300
on good terms with Microsoft but this wasn’t&nbsp;
enough to cover for Microsoft’s shortcomings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:14.160
To make things worse, developer support for&nbsp;
Windows phones was atrocious. Developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:19.980
would usually just start with iOS then move&nbsp;
onto Android and then just skip Windows. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:24.360
given that 3rd party apps make the smartphone&nbsp;
experience for most people, a lack of 3rd party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:31.260
apps made the Windows Phone an absolute no go.&nbsp;
If this was anyone else, this right here is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:35.880
their journey within the smartphone market&nbsp;
would’ve come to an end. But, since we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.898
talking about Microsoft, they would have the&nbsp;
money and resources to throw a last hail mary.

00:05:39.898 --> 00:05:40.440
A HAIL MARY:&nbsp;

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:52.980
Given the topic of the video, I’m sure you guessed&nbsp;
that Nokia was the hail mary but this actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:59.520
played out a lot differently than you might think.&nbsp;
You see, Microsoft didn’t just move to buy Nokia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:06.360
immediately. They didn’t really want to own Nokia,&nbsp;
they just wanted a rock solid partnership and as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:12.780
such, they would send a trojan horse to Nokia in&nbsp;
late 2010 in the form of Stephen Elop. Elop was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:17.340
the president of Microsoft’s business division&nbsp;
which at the time was producing record profits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:24.660
so he was in a pretty comfortable position. But&nbsp;
for some reason, in September of 2010, Elop would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:31.080
take a job offer from Nokia to become their CEO.&nbsp;
From the very beginning, this deal seemed fishy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:36.600
Why would the leader of the largest division of&nbsp;
the largest software company in the world leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:46.080
to join a dying giant? And as for Nokia, Elop&nbsp;
was their first non finnish CEO in their 145 year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:51.180
history and to give Elop this job, they had fire&nbsp;
a man who had been with the company for 30 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:57.780
Surely, there was more to the story than just&nbsp;
Stephen being a good candidate. Was Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:04.500
and Nokia secretly planning a partnership to take&nbsp;
on Google and Apple? Well yes, that’s exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:09.000
what they were planning. Just a couple months&nbsp;
after Stephen became CEO, they would publicly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:15.240
announce that Nokia was going to exclusively sell&nbsp;
Windows Phones. Nokia was betting it all. Instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:20.100
going down the proven path of selling Androids,&nbsp;
Nokia had chosen to take a gamble with Windows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:26.160
but their allegiance to Microsoft went much deeper&nbsp;
than just running Windows. Nokia would basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:32.220
become a subsidiary of Micrososft supporting&nbsp;
everything Microsoft. For example, they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:37.260
choose to integrate Bing instead of Google, they&nbsp;
would integrate Bing Maps instead of Google Maps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:42.420
and they would ditch their own app store in favor&nbsp;
of the Windows Phone Store. They basically became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:48.240
the go to Windows phone maker and they were&nbsp;
decently rewarded for it. Unit sales would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:54.360
rocket to nearly 10 million per quarter but in the&nbsp;
grand scheme of things, this was still a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.360 --> 00:08:00.900
step down for the phone giant that was Nokia. In&nbsp;
fact, calling it a step down was an understatement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:07.560
as Nokia was getting obliterated. During, Elop’s&nbsp;
tenure, Nokia’s stock price crashed 62%, their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:12.900
mobile phone market share haved, their smartphone&nbsp;
market share fell from 33% to 3%, and the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:20.220
lost €4.9 billion. Likely the best evidence of&nbsp;
just how bad things were was this leaked memo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:26.880
from Elop. Elop would compare Nokia to a burning&nbsp;
oil platform and claim that Nokia had to jump into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:32.880
the icy water to have any chance at survival. This&nbsp;
metaphorical jump was partnering with Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:39.120
and going all in on the Windows Phone but this&nbsp;
wasn’t quite playing out as expected. What Nokia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:46.020
needed was a savior, and who better to save them&nbsp;
than Microsoft. Nokia had bet it all with Windows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:50.820
so it was only fair that Micorosft returned&nbsp;
the favor and bet it all with Nokia. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:55.320
while that sounded nice on paper, Elop knew that&nbsp;
he needed something more than just good faith to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:01.260
get Microsoft to buy into a growing money pit.&nbsp;
And with that began the bid to sell off Nokia.

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:07.620
BLACKMAILED:&nbsp;

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:12.960
Elop didn’t just jump straight into threatening&nbsp;
Microsoft. Rather it’s likely that he started off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.640
with a Motorola sort of approach. If you didn’t&nbsp;
know, around this same time, Google would acquire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:25.740
Motorola for a ridiculous $12.5 billion. On paper,&nbsp;
this was a terrible deal as Google would end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:31.440
losing billions on this purchase but that’s just&nbsp;
on paper. The reality is that Google actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:38.160
gained something invaluable from Motorola: their&nbsp;
over 20,000 granted and pending patents. It’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.660
really long story and I have a full video about&nbsp;
it but essentially Google would use these patents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:47.640
to save Android from litigation from Apple and&nbsp;
Microsoft. Seeing this, Elop likely thought about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:52.800
pulling the same thing over on Microsoft given&nbsp;
that Nokia had way more patents. Over 90,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:57.960
patents to be precise. But there was one&nbsp;
fatal flaw with this strategy. Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:02.865
wasn’t desperate to save themselves from a lawsuit&nbsp;
like Google was. I mean, they were literally the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.865 --> 00:10:07.920
ones that were suing Google with patents that they&nbsp;
already had, so it was back to the drawing board.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:13.860
And that’s when Elop realized that while he may&nbsp;
not be able to bribe Microsoft into buying Nokia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:19.620
he could force their hand. You see, as Nokia&nbsp;
became the go to Windows phone manufacturer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:26.100
other manufacturers had slowly stepped away.&nbsp;
In fact, by 2013, Nokia controlled 90% of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:33.300
Windows phone market. So, why not threaten to move&nbsp;
to Android? And that’s exactly how things played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:38.760
out. In early 2013, Nokia would publicly announce&nbsp;
that they were considering a move to Android. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:44.340
be honest, this was an easy out for Microsoft.&nbsp;
They could’ve just let Nokia move to Android,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:49.920
accept that they had already lost the smartphone&nbsp;
wars, and move on with their lives. But instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.960
Microsoft would freak out that they were about to&nbsp;
lose their last chance to win smartphones and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.960 --> 00:11:01.320
would end up prematurely buying Nokia’s phone&nbsp;
business for $7.2 billion. This was honestly a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:06.300
genius move by Elop as he would brilliantly make&nbsp;
his way back to Microsoft as an executive after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:12.300
having a terrible run at Nokia. In fact, Elop is&nbsp;
still at Microsoft serving as their executive vice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:18.480
president. So, looks like things played out pretty&nbsp;
well for Elop but the same could not be said for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:24.420
Microsoft or Nokia’s phone business. You see, just&nbsp;
a couple months after Microsoft acquired Nokia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:31.680
Microsoft would get a new CEO: Satya Nadella. From&nbsp;
day one, Satya saw Nokia for exactly what it was:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:37.920
a desperate attempt by Steve Ballmer to salvage&nbsp;
an already lost industry. So instead of focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.180
his efforts on Windows Phones, Satya would&nbsp;
focus on the Azure and enterprise side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:47.340
Microsoft. And once he had enough results to show&nbsp;
shareholders from that side of Microsoft he would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:52.200
ruthelssly incinerate Microsoft’s phone business.&nbsp;
He would sell off the remaining parts of Nokia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:58.260
lay off thousands of Nokia employees, and&nbsp;
write off $7.6 billion in losses. Keep in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:03.540
they only paid $7.2 billion for the business&nbsp;
so they essentially wrote off the entire amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:08.580
and another $400 million on top of that.&nbsp;
Yeah I don’t think it’s controversial to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:13.980
say that this was by far Microsoft's worst&nbsp;
acquisition and its all because they let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:19.140
the threat of Nokia switching to Android get to&nbsp;
their head. Unlike Microsoft’s bet with Nokia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:24.600
Google’s bet with Motorola may be their best bet&nbsp;
of all time. Check out this video to learn more.

